import re, sys
p = sys.argv[1]
s = open(p).read()
R = [
("""No amendment. Market has no control that removes an equation without a submission: the only way an order leaves the row is **Submit**, and the two you did not choose keep their places and keep occupying two of your three choices. Choosing is not skipping — and §5 shows the difference is the whole economy: a row whose unchosen equations vanish *is* a free skip button, and at k = 3 with the shipped kind bias 98% of bot runs pass 100 submissions, where the standing row ends every run. If a later variant lets an order *expire* without a submission, that is a priced pass and needs this clause amended; it is measured in §5 and not proposed.""",
"""No amendment. No control removes an order without a submission: an order leaves the row only by **Submit**, and the two you did not choose keep their places — and two of your three choices. Choosing is not skipping, and §5 shows the difference is the whole economy: a row whose unchosen equations vanish *is* a free skip button (k = 3, shipped kind bias: 98% of bot runs pass 100 submissions), while the standing row ends every run. An *expiry* rule would be a priced pass and need this clause amended; §5 measures it and does not propose it."""),
("""No amendment; the ruling's premise moves slightly and the move is measured. The deliberate miss stays the only manual discard; what Market adds is the choice of *which* order to miss, so the player picks the price (a 1-digit order costs one tile, a 2-digit order two). At a full hand the bot's realised miss cost falls from 1.80 (one order, kind off) to 1.64 (k = 3); the economy figures below already include that drop. The miss also gains a second use: paying it on a dead order frees its place.""",
"""No amendment; its premise shifts measurably. The deliberate miss stays the only manual discard, but choosing *which* order to miss lets the player pick its price (one tile or two): the bot's full-hand miss cost falls from 1.80 (one order, kind off) to 1.64 (k = 3), and every figure in §5 includes that drop. The miss also gains a use: paying it on a dead order frees its place."""),
("""**Housekeeping amendments.** §1.1 and §1.3 name two modes and the title mode select (§1.10) holds two cards; §1.10 `answering` "Equation and exact answer-slot count" becomes the three-order row plus the active order's slots; §1.11 gains ← / → in `answering`; ui-i18n §1.14 gains copy keys and §1.15 the prefix `ozterisk Market — Rounds: {totalRounds}`. **Motion (ui-i18n §1.12):** none new — the order dealt into the vacated place rises with `10b`'s `oz-round-rise`, which already fires once per arriving equation; the waiting orders do not move. **Layout:** the row takes the equation's place in "HUD → equation/slots → phase action → rail → inventory"; three orders plus two gaps must fit the 281px budget at the 320px gate — about 88px per order with the numerals stepped down, unverified.""",
"""**Housekeeping amendments.** §1.1/§1.3 and the title's mode select (§1.10) gain a third mode; §1.10 `answering` swaps "Equation and exact answer-slot count" for the row plus the active order's slots; §1.11 gains ← / →; ui-i18n §1.14 gains copy keys and §1.15 the prefix `ozterisk Market — Rounds: {totalRounds}`. **Motion (§1.12):** none new — the dealt order rises with `10b`'s `oz-round-rise`; waiting orders never move. **Layout:** the row takes the equation's place; three orders must fit the 281px budget at the 320px gate (about 88px each, smaller numerals) — unverified."""),
("""**Precedents.** Patchwork offers "the three patches in front of the neutral token", and the token then moves "next to the chosen patch" [1] — the closest structural relative: a window of three, and nothing removes the patches you pass over (my reading of the rule). Century: Spice Road makes "later ones" cost "cubes on all earlier cards", and "the others shift down" [2] — reaching past the front of the row is taxed. Splendor shows four face-up cards per level and refills one the moment it is bought or reserved; reserving (at most three) is its hold [3]. Ticket to Ride guards the generosity of an open market: a face-up locomotive ends your draw, and three face-up locomotives discard and replace all five [4]. The Tetris hold keeps a piece out until the swapped piece locks [5] — in ozterisk terms, a standing row of two, measured below. Skipping is priced where it exists: Balatro lets a Small or Big Blind be skipped for a Tag, forfeiting its cash, and the Boss Blind cannot be skipped [6]; Overcooked 2's kitchen tickets — "failed or missed orders cause you to lose money" [7]. Slay the Spire lays encounters out in "a branching structure", elites that "offer enhanced rewards" [8]. What they share: every open market either keeps the untaken items in play or charges for passing them by. Market keeps them, and §5 shows that is enough — no tax, timer or expiry.""",
"""**Precedents.** Patchwork offers "the three patches in front of the neutral token", which then moves "next to the chosen patch" [1]: a window of three in which passed-over patches stay (my reading). Century: Spice Road taxes reaching past the front — "later ones" cost "cubes on all earlier cards", and "the others shift down" [2]. Splendor keeps four face-up cards a level, refilled when one is bought or reserved; reserving (at most three) is its hold [3]. Ticket to Ride guards an open market's generosity: a face-up locomotive ends your draw, and three face-up locomotives replace all five [4]. Tetris's hold keeps a piece out until the swapped one locks [5] — a standing row of two, measured below. Balatro prices a skip (a Small or Big Blind traded for a Tag, forfeiting its cash; the Boss Blind cannot be skipped) [6], as Overcooked 2 prices a missed ticket: "failed or missed orders cause you to lose money" [7]. Slay the Spire lays encounters out in "a branching structure", elites offering "enhanced rewards" [8]. Every open market either keeps untaken items in play or charges for passing them; Market keeps them, and §5 shows that is enough — no tax, timer or expiry."""),
("""**Where it sits.** The naive model says choice is fatal: with three orders visible and the spellable one always answerable, effective `b` ≈ 1 − (1 − b)³, far over the 0.631 cliff. That holds only if unchosen orders vanish — a free skip. If they stay, they **clog**: the orders you could not spell are exactly the ones left in the row. By submission 11 about two of the three are dead, and late in a run Market's drift matches Endless's. The counterweight is conservation — an order leaves only when it is submitted — and no other dial is needed.""",
"""**Where it sits.** The naive model says choice is fatal: always answering the spellable one of three gives `b` ≈ 1 − (1 − b)³, far past 0.631. That holds only if unchosen orders vanish — a free skip. If they stay, they **clog**: the orders you could not spell are exactly the ones left behind. By submission 11 about two of three are dead, and late in a run Market's drift matches Endless's. The counterweight is conservation — an order leaves only when submitted — and no other dial is needed."""),
("""**Instrument and conditions.** `market-sim.ts` (work folder; run with the brief's runner) imports the real `generateEquation`, `generateKindEquation` (shipped 0.2) and `generateRewardTiles` (Endless digits) and re-implements only the round loop, since the reducer holds one equation. Spellability uses a digit-count predicate verified against the real `canConstruct` on 92,160 (hand, product) cases with 0 disagreements; a deliberately wrong predicate disagrees on 8,192, so the check can fail. One bot policy throughout: answer a spellable order (prefer the one that leaves the others spellable, then the older); with none spellable, miss the cheapest order using the least useful tiles; on overflow, discard to keep open orders spellable, then digit spread, then the most-copied digit. 2,000 runs per row, cap 2,000 submissions, unless marked; full-hand statistics skip each run's first 10 submissions.""",
"""**Instrument and conditions.** `market-sim.ts` (work folder, the brief's runner) imports the real `generateEquation`, `generateKindEquation` (shipped 0.2) and `generateRewardTiles` and re-implements only the round loop, since the reducer holds one equation. Its digit-count spell check agrees with the real `canConstruct` on all 92,160 (hand, product) cases tried; a deliberately wrong predicate disagrees on 8,192, so the check can fail. One bot policy throughout: answer a spellable order (prefer the one that leaves others spellable, then the oldest); with none, miss the cheapest with the least useful tiles; on overflow, keep open orders spellable, then digit spread, shedding the most-copied digit. 2,000 runs a row, cap 2,000 submissions unless marked; full-hand statistics skip each run's first 10 submissions."""),
("""**Calibration.** Engine, with the hand redrawn uniformly before every draw: `b(10)` = 0.482 (repo 0.48), `b'(10)` = 0.581 with the kind bias (repo 0.58), full-hand drift −0.454. The repo's −0.41 multiplies the pool-average miss cost 1.71; at a full hand misses skew to 2-digit products and cost 1.81, and the analytic drift with that conditional cost is −0.460, which the engine matches — so at a full hand the true zero-drift point is 1.81 / 2.81 = 0.644, and 0.631 is slightly conservative. Endless baseline: the same engine at k = 1 with the fixed bot gives median 18 rounds and full-hand drift −0.09. **Negative control:** zero rewards — no run outlasts 10 submissions. **Positive control:** a free row at k = 5 pins 100% of runs at the cap.""",
"""**Calibration.** With the hand redrawn uniformly before every draw, the engine gives `b(10)` = 0.482 (repo 0.48), `b'(10)` = 0.581 (repo 0.58) and full-hand drift −0.454. The repo's −0.41 multiplies the pool-average miss cost 1.71; at a full hand misses skew to 2-digit products and cost 1.81, and the analytic drift with that cost, −0.460, is what the engine reproduces — so 0.631 is slightly conservative (the full-hand zero is 1.81 / 2.81 = 0.644). At k = 1 the fixed bot plays Endless to median 18 rounds, full-hand drift −0.09. **Negative control:** zero rewards — no run outlasts 10 submissions. **Positive control:** a free row at k = 5 pins 100% of runs at the cap."""),
("""**Why the full-hand column misleads.** The standing row's full-hand drift is positive, yet its runs end: the row is a second state variable. Full-hand rounds cluster early, before the row has clogged; in the chart its spellable share falls from 0.70 to 0.24 by submissions 11–15 while the free row holds 0.48. For a mode with a standing row, the cliff test has to be run over time, not at the ceiling — which `balance.test.ts`'s analytic, single-state model could not see; a Market dial would need a simulated guard, which that file deliberately avoids.""",
"""**Why the full-hand column misleads.** The standing row's full-hand drift is positive, yet its runs end, because the row is a second state variable: full-hand rounds cluster early, before it clogs. In the chart its spellable share falls from 0.70 to 0.24 by submissions 11–15 while the free row holds 0.48. A standing row's cliff test must run over time, not at the ceiling — which `balance.test.ts`'s single-state analytic model cannot see; a Market dial would need a simulated guard, which that file deliberately avoids."""),
("""Market at k = 3, kind off, digits only; Endless's capacity, rack, overflow and HUD unchanged.

- **Reuse:** `gameReducer`'s `SUBMIT_CORRECT`, `SUBMIT_INCORRECT`, `TOGGLE_DISCARD` and `CLEAR_SELECTION` work untouched because they act on `state.equation`; `generateEquation`, `generateRewardTiles`, `createInitialInventory`, `sortTiles`, `getAnswerLength`, `getCapacity` (endless branch); `EquationBoard` (one instance per order), `AnswerSlots`, `TileInventory`, `OverflowControls`, `GameHud`, `CapacityMeter`, `services/sharing.ts`.
- **Add:** `GameMode` `"market"`; `orders: Equation[]` and `activeOrder` in `GameState`, keeping `equation` equal to the active order so existing cases and components keep working; `SELECT_ORDER { index }` (answering only; returns placed tiles); `NEXT_ROUND` replacing only `orders[activeOrder]` and checking loss against the shortest open order; `App.tsx` dealing three orders at start/restart and one per round; an `OrderRow` component of three toggle buttons (`aria-pressed`, a spoken label per order); ← / → in `useGameKeyboard`; i18n keys, the third mode card, the share prefix. Tests first: selecting returns tiles; submit resolves the active order; next round fills only the vacated place; loss uses the shortest open order.

A cheaper first test of the thesis is the hold variant, k = 2.""",
"""Market at k = 3, kind off, digits only; Endless's capacity, rack, overflow and HUD unchanged.

- **Reuse:** `gameReducer`'s `SUBMIT_CORRECT`, `SUBMIT_INCORRECT`, `TOGGLE_DISCARD` and `CLEAR_SELECTION`, untouched (they act on `state.equation`); `generateEquation`, `generateRewardTiles`, `createInitialInventory`, `sortTiles`, `getAnswerLength`, `getCapacity`; `EquationBoard` (once per order), `AnswerSlots`, `TileInventory`, `OverflowControls`, `GameHud`, `CapacityMeter`, `services/sharing.ts`.
- **Add:** `GameMode` `"market"`; `orders: Equation[]` and `activeOrder` in `GameState`, with `equation` kept equal to the active order; `SELECT_ORDER { index }` (answering only; returns placed tiles); `NEXT_ROUND` replacing only `orders[activeOrder]` and checking loss against the shortest open order; `App.tsx` dealing three orders at start and one per round; an `OrderRow` of three `aria-pressed` toggle buttons; ← / → in `useGameKeyboard`; i18n keys, a third mode card, the share prefix. Tests first: select returns tiles; submit resolves the active order; next round fills only the vacated place; loss uses the shortest order.

A cheaper first test of the thesis is the hold variant, k = 2."""),
("""**The choice thins exactly when it matters.** From submission 16 on, more than half of all rounds offer no answerable order and only 16% offer a choice between two; the late row is one live order and two dead ones. Read kindly that is a ledger of your own debts; read unkindly it is clutter that says the game is stuck. Second, reading cost: three products per round may slow play enough that players answer whichever order is already active and never compare — Market would be Endless with extra reading, and the "choice they must make" that `decisions.md` warned against. The economy cannot detect either; both are about feel.

**Kill test.** Five players, three runs each, after one Endless run. Drop the direction if, in rounds where two orders are answerable, most players answer the active one without looking at the others (no switching); or if players describe the waiting orders as "stuck" or "broken" rather than as something they chose; or if median round time more than doubles against Endless while players say they would rather be dealt one equation. Keep it if players deliberately strand or clear orders and can say why.""",
"""**The choice thins exactly when it matters.** From submission 16, more than half of all rounds offer no answerable order and only 16% offer two: the late row is one live order and two dead ones — a ledger of your own debts, or clutter that says the game is stuck. And three products per round may slow play until players simply answer whichever order is active — Endless with extra reading, the "choice they must make" `decisions.md` warned against. The economy cannot detect either; both are about feel.

**Kill test.** Five players, three runs each, after one Endless run. Drop the direction if, in rounds where two orders are answerable, most players answer the active one without looking at the others; if players call the waiting orders "stuck" or "broken" rather than something they chose; or if median round time more than doubles against Endless while players say they would rather be dealt one equation. Keep it if players deliberately strand or clear orders and can say why."""),
]
for old, new in R:
    if s.count(old) != 1:
        sys.exit(f"MISMATCH ({s.count(old)}): {old[:70]!r}")
    s = s.replace(old, new)
open(p, "w").write(s)
body = re.sub(r"```.*?```", "", s, flags=re.S).split("## Sources")[0]
lines = [l for l in body.splitlines() if not l.startswith("|") and not l.startswith("![") and not l.startswith("#")]
print("prose words:", len(" ".join(lines).split()))
